# Line endings, decided per repo rather than per machine.
#
# Without this file, each clone's core.autocrlf setting decides. On Windows that
# is typically true, which is what produced two long-running annoyances here:
#
#   1. "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" on every
#      single commit.
#   2. data/personalityQuiz.ts sitting permanently as " M" in git status while
#      git diff showed nothing, because the worktree copy had been saved with
#      CRLF while the committed blob was LF. Content identical, bytes not.
#
# text=auto  git decides what is text, and always stores it LF in the repo
# eol=lf     and checks it out LF too, on every platform
#
# LF everywhere is the norm for the JS/TS toolchain, and VS Code handles LF
# files on Windows without complaint.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries git must never convert. text=auto usually detects these, but being
# explicit means a corrupt file can never be blamed on line-ending conversion.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.pdf   binary
